{"type":"message","id":"a92f53d4","parentId":"5e378796","timestamp":"2026-05-22T08:22:19.415Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_ZMdpA8aP3iIIhqpy6euMPGiu","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/crypto-article-raw.md","content":"META\n- SEO Title: Bitcoin reserve bill targets $25B US stash\n- Meta Description: US lawmakers introduced the ARMA bill to lock a Bitcoin reserve into law, covering 328,372 BTC, audits, custody rules, and self-custody rights.\n- URL Slug: bitcoin-reserve-bill\n- Categories: Regulation, Latest News\n- Primary Keyword: Bitcoin reserve\n- Secondary Keywords: strategic Bitcoin reserve, ARMA bill, US Treasury Bitcoin, self-custody rights\n\n---\n\n# Bitcoin reserve bill targets $25B US stash\n\nBitcoin reserve policy moved back to the center of Washington this week after lawmakers introduced the American Reserve Modernization Act, or ARMA. The proposal would convert a still-fragile executive initiative into statute, which matters because statutory rules are harder to reverse and easier for markets to price. Early reporting from [Decrypt](https://decrypt.co/368662/proposed-arma-bill-aims-enshrine-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-law) and [CryptoSlate](https://cryptoslate.com/us-lawmakers-push-new-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-act-to-secure-25-billion-federal-stash/) points to the same core message: Congress is being asked to treat Bitcoin as a long-duration reserve asset, not a temporary political headline.\n\nFor traders, the market impact is less about immediate buying pressure and more about a change in policy floor. If the federal government commits to hold existing Bitcoin for at least 20 years, that reduces the chance that seized coins re-enter the market through periodic auctions. It also turns sovereign custody into a live policy category, which could affect expectations for other governments and large institutions.\n\nThe bill arrives with bipartisan branding, led by Republican Nick Begich and Democratic co-lead Jared Golden. That does not make passage easy, but it does improve the signal quality. In a policy area that often splits along ideology, bipartisan sponsorship tells the market this idea is moving beyond campaign language and into a framework that lawmakers want on the books.\n\n## Why the Bitcoin reserve proposal matters now\n\nARMA aims to lock a strategic Bitcoin reserve into federal law and place it inside the Treasury Department. The proposal also creates a separate digital asset stockpile for non-Bitcoin crypto already held by the government. That split is important because it treats Bitcoin as its own category while still giving the government a framework for everything else it controls through seizures, penalties, and forfeitures.\n\nThe timing matters because the executive-order route has obvious limits. An administration can create a reserve in principle, but agencies still need clear authority, custody rules, accounting treatment, and reporting standards before a market can treat the policy as durable. A bill does more than express support. It can define holding periods, reporting duties, audit requirements, and congressional oversight in a way an executive order cannot fully guarantee.\n\n### From executive action to statutory backing\n\nThis is the main shift traders should watch. The White House can set direction, but Congress decides whether that direction survives the next election cycle. ARMA is trying to remove some of that political uncertainty by giving the reserve the weight of law.\n\nThat changes the discussion from symbolic support to operational commitment. If a future administration wanted to liquidate the reserve quickly, statutory guardrails could make that harder. Markets tend to value policies that narrow discretion, especially when the asset involved has deep ideological and geopolitical meaning.\n\n## What sits inside the reserve and how it could affect supply\n\nAccording to the reporting cited by supporters of the bill, the US government currently holds 328,372 BTC worth more than $25 billion. That figure gives ARMA immediate scale. This is not a proposal that starts from zero. It starts from an existing federal stash that has usually been viewed as inventory for potential disposal.\n\nIf those holdings are moved into a formal Bitcoin reserve and subject to a 20-year minimum holding period, the supply effect is straightforward. Coins that might have been sold over time would instead be treated as strategic assets. That does not change Bitcoin's total supply, but it can change assumptions about liquid supply, especially when traders are assessing long-term sell-side pressure from large entities.\n\n### The 20-year hold and auction risk\n\nThe 20-year rule is the most market-relevant line in the bill. Governments have historically auctioned seized Bitcoin, and those sales can become reference points for how the state views the asset. ARMA flips that posture. Instead of converting Bitcoin into dollars as a default outcome, the proposal says the asset itself may be worth holding as a sovereign reserve.\n\nFor market structure, that matters in three ways:\n- It lowers expectations of periodic federal selling.\n- It strengthens the narrative of Bitcoin as a reserve asset rather than contraband inventory.\n- It creates a benchmark other jurisdictions may study if they want state-level or national crypto reserves.\n\nNone of that guarantees a price rally. Congress can still stall the bill, and political opposition is real. But the proposal does create a new category of tailwind, one tied to supply custody and time horizon rather than short-term ETF flows or leverage conditions.\n\n## Transparency, acquisition rules, and the bigger policy read\n\nA reserve is only credible if the market can see how it is run. ARMA addresses that by calling for quarterly public proof-of-reserve style reporting, independent third-party audits, and congressional oversight. Those measures are not cosmetic. They are aimed at the exact trust problem that surrounds government crypto custody: the market wants to know what is held, where it sits, and whether the numbers can be verified.\n\nThe bill also directs a study into budget-neutral acquisition strategies. That is a notable detail because it leaves open the idea of expanding holdings later without linking the plan to tax increases or deficit spending. In practice, that means the current proposal is less aggressive than calls for a million-Bitcoin buying program, but it still keeps the door open for future accumulation if lawmakers can justify the method.\n\n### Self-custody rights and the message to the market\n\nOne underappreciated feature is the bill's explicit protection for lawful self-custody. That clause broadens the proposal beyond reserve management and speaks to the policy environment around personal ownership. For traders and long-term holders, it suggests supporters of the bill want to pair state accumulation with a friendlier domestic framework for private users.\n\nThat combination matters because it reduces the risk of a contradictory message. A government that wants a Bitcoin reserve while restricting lawful personal custody would send a confused signal. A government that protects both is saying Bitcoin can function as a strategic asset at the sovereign level and as legitimate property at the individual level.\n\nThe broader read is that Washington's pro-Bitcoin wing is becoming more organized around infrastructure, not slogans. Clearer reserve rules, clearer custody rules, and clearer reporting standards all help move the debate from ideology toward implementation. Traders should still treat passage odds with caution, but they should not ignore the directional signal.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe Bitcoin reserve debate is no longer just about whether officials like the asset. ARMA asks whether the United States should hold Bitcoin as a long-term strategic balance sheet asset, publish reserve data, and reduce the chance of future federal liquidations. For traders, that makes this bill more than a political headline. It is a supply, custody, and policy signal that could reshape how sovereign participation in Bitcoin is priced over time.\n\n## FAQs\n\n**1. What is the ARMA bill trying to do?**\n\nThe bill would place a strategic Bitcoin reserve into federal law and assign Treasury to manage it. It would also create a separate stockpile for non-Bitcoin digital assets already held by the government. The goal is to replace ad hoc handling with a durable legal framework.\n\n**2. Why does the 20-year holding period matter for the market?**\n\nA mandatory holding period reduces the expectation that the government will sell large amounts of Bitcoin through auctions. That matters because federal sales add potential supply to the market. Removing or delaying that supply can change long-term assumptions even if nothing happens immediately.\n\n**3. Does this mean the US will start buying more Bitcoin right away?**\n\nNo, not based on the current reporting. The bill studies budget-neutral ways to expand holdings, but it does not guarantee immediate purchases. Right now, the clearest near-term effect is on custody and treatment of Bitcoin the government already controls.\n\n**4. How large is the federal Bitcoin position today?**\n\nReporting tied to the bill says the US holds 328,372 BTC worth more than $25 billion. That makes the proposal meaningful from day one because the reserve would start with a sizable asset base. The exact value will move with Bitcoin's price.\n\n**5. What does self-custody protection add to the bill?**\n\nIt signals that supporters want a reserve policy that does not conflict with private ownership rights. For the market, that is relevant because it suggests a broader pro-Bitcoin legal stance rather than a narrow government-only approach. It also helps frame Bitcoin as lawful property, not just a seized asset category.\n\n**Sources:**\n- Decrypt: https://decrypt.co/368662/proposed-arma-bill-aims-enshrine-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-law\n- CryptoSlate: https://cryptoslate.com/us-lawmakers-push-new-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-act-to-secure-25-billion-federal-stash/\n- CryptoFox: https://cryptofox.news/news/us-lawmakers-introduce-bill-to-establish-strategic-bitcoin-reserve\n\n[Word Count: 1074]\n"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":12471,"output":2035,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":14506,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779438119277,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DiFQmfhSEjmJFpTyEUd7hWNjoJGfT"}}
